WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
Hi Reef. Welcome to Summit One.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.640
Get curious, discover clever ideas, and become a more

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.160
confident problem solver.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.700
Your adventure starts with one small step.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.710
You have three exciting destinations: the Australian

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.930
Mathematics Competition, MAWA's Problem Solving Program, and

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.570
Rossmoyne's Honours Mathematics programme.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.470
Each opens a different door into maths.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.240
The national AMC began in nineteen seventy-eight.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.860
Dedicated primary papers have been running for over twenty

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.940
years. And in twenty twenty-seven, Years One and Two join the

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.920
standard competition through the new Early Primary division.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.720
The adventure keeps growing.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.810
Here's a glimpse of the ideas you'll learn.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.650
What is the last digit of seven to the power of

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.870
two thousand and twenty-seven?

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.710
That enormous number hides a surprisingly short repeating

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:50.210
pattern.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:55.070
The last digits cycle through seven, nine, three, one.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.830
Every four powers, the cycle repeats.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.630
Two thousand and twenty-seven leaves remainder three when

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:02.630
divided by four.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.130
So choose the third position: the last digit is three.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.270
MAWA, the Mathematical Association of Western Australia, began

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.070
in nineteen fifty-eight.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.910
Its Problem Solving Program dates from nineteen ninety-two.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.450
It develops careful reasoning and clear explanations.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.170
You'll learn to ask why an idea works.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.420
Can twenty-three dominoes cover a six-by-eight board with

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.580
opposite corners removed?

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.560
Colour it like a chessboard.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.900
Each domino covers one blue and one gold square.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.980
But the board has twenty-two blue squares and twenty-four gold

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:41.140
ones. Impossible! Colouring turns a puzzle into a proof.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.940
Rossmoyne's Honours Mathematics is a selective-entry

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.400
programme, with applicants in Year Six.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.920
Students explore beyond the usual curriculum.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.440
They're known as the Fermat Club, with collaborative problem

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.680
solving and an AMC training camp.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.180
You can prepare to think flexibly and explain your ideas.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.090
You'll build number sense, visual thinking, and flexible

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.910
reasoning. Draw a diagram.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.490
Work backwards. Break a big puzzle into smaller pieces.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.560
Each lesson introduces an idea and shows how it works.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.800
Quick choices sharpen your thinking.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.440
For two, six, twelve, twenty, thirty, what comes next?

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.840
The gaps grow by two: add twelve to get forty-two.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.020
Then tackle a deeper challenge.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.460
You'll learn to recognise what stays unchanged.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.760
Rotate a domino: it still covers one of each colour.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.640
A familiar idea can unlock a completely different-looking

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.120
problem.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.870
Getting stuck is part of the work.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.510
Try a smaller example.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:41.590
Make a sketch.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:42.890
Check a clue.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.790
Every careful attempt gives you something to learn.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.400
Practice papers let you rehearse: read carefully, choose a

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.440
strategy, manage your time, and check your answers.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.120
Afterwards, use the worked solutions to improve.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.470
When exam day arrives, take a breath.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:00.850
Start with what you know.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.370
You can move on and return to a tough question.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.290
Aim high, one thoughtful step at a time.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.320
You don't need all the answers today, Reef.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:10.600
Bring your curiosity.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.300
Build your toolkit.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.100
Enjoy those aha moments.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.380
Your next discovery starts here.

